883} {:answer/id #uuid "3665dcf6-e972-5c94-af31-9f3479be32eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{216(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc424cc2-ff16-52f4-a49c-4b123a800081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365882} :answer-field/id #uuid "211ca2e4-7757-5188-bf85-29952b195db0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20ffe3-ae1f-5f5e-a04b-30196b3dc15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365886} {:answer/id #uuid "c524d8fa-426f-54d4-8d44-a208c1c9b83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d20ffe3-ae1f-5f5e-a04b-30196b3dc15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365886} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9d6718-40ef-5a86-a94c-716236550d32" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "453e4017-fcd5-5827-9f9b-4883dd0cbdc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343251" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=12x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-2∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n12x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ 12x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
